###############################################################################
# Q11 -- Computational resources
###############################################################################

# Time per MD iteration (Table 2) for the 2,160-atom alpha-Al2O3 cell of
# the stoichiometric performance test, NequIP on one Tesla P100 GPU; the
# per-atom time is the iteration time divided by 2,160. Classical
# potentials: Vashishta 0.02 s, Matsui 0.06 s, ReaxFF 0.62 s on the same
# set-up. No training times are given.

###############################################################################
# Q12 -- Gaps and ambiguities (prose; not triples)
###############################################################################

# - Energy RMSEs are stated in eV/atom to two decimals and converted to meV/atom; stoichiometric energies are relative to alpha-Al2O3 (Eq. 1) and non-stoichiometric ones are formation energies relative to fcc Al and O2 (Eq. 2).
# - The Table 2 timing column is not split by test; it is attached to the stoichiometric results, whose performance cell (2,160 atoms) is defined in Sec. 2.3 (the non-stoichiometric test used a 4,368-atom cell).
# - The default NequIP hyperparameter values are given only by reference to the developers' training file (Materials Cloud); the other hyperparameter scans of Fig. 6 and Table S5 are figure-only. The DFT settings of the database are those of Ref. [41].
# - Author affiliations are not encoded (ORCIDs are those deposited with the DOI metadata, source: crossref).
# - Evaluation roles and data, base models, inference timings with their system sizes, and the source location of each value are recorded in the encoding input (artifacts/kg/encoding/specs/gramatte2024.yaml) until the schema has terms for them.

